pgpid (0.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  [ Mnêmê ]
  * pgpid: the C tool, in C over gpgme, arrives from the foodjis repository
    where it was written. Twenty-four actions, replacing bl-pgpid and
    bl-pgpkey action for action.
  * Nine message catalogues; French complete for the messages themselves.
  * Build-depends on gettext, help2man and pandoc, which the packaging needed
    already and did not say. Not on libgpgme-dev nor pkgconf: the keyring is
    read by asking gpg now, and the binary links nothing but libc.
  * Depends on ${shlibs:Depends}: there is a compiled binary now.
  * The tools the printing and scanning actions call are Recommends rather
    than Depends — pgpid reads certificates without any of them.
  * Reads the keyring by asking gpg rather than through gpgme, which could
    not see user attributes and answered questions we had not asked.
  * trustdb: one action where ownertrust and update_trustdb were two; export
    produces what import consumes, and import weighs every line against what
    this machine already says rather than overwriting it.
  * certify, cert_revoke, cert_get --recurse, cert_sigs, cert_email
    --set-primary, cert_property expire, secret_del, token_del, token_cache:
    the actions the shell had, and several it did not.
  * system_adduser / system_deluser / system_confhome / system_users /
    system_admins: a certificate opens and closes a local account.
  * gen_u4 takes a civil status typed or read off a passport, in one action.
  * The sixteen helps, in nine languages, saying what the shell's said.
  * The program completes itself: bash-completion asks the binary.
  * cert_get asks the keyserver the way HKP is asked — one op=get request,
    whatever the search is — instead of only reaching out for an address or a
    fingerprint. An entity identifier is looked up by its body, which is what
    the old spelling (u4=…) and the new one (u4…) share, so a certificate
    minted before the separator went away is found again.
  * secret_scan reads a camera again, as bl-pgpkey scan did: --camera, and
    pgpid-qrscan falls back to it when no sheet is named. Which camera is
    asked of the kernel rather than left to zbarcam's default, and named when
    there is more than one; the size is the largest the camera streams up to
    1920x1080, since 640x480 cannot resolve a secret printed as a single QR
    code. --cameras answers the list on its own, for a caller with nobody to
    ask.
  * gen_u4 says when the reference transliteration did not take a name as it
    was typed — MÜLLER as MULLER — which the shell said and this did not, and
    --verify shows the whole civil status for correction before computing.
  * secret_totoken sets the codes it announces. It called token_code without
    --replace, which only verifies, so a freshly written card kept its factory
    PIN and Admin code while a random pair was printed. token_code now refuses
    a new code given without --replace.
  * secret_totoken asks before wiping a card that carries something, instead
    of refusing outright, and asks nothing of a blank one.
  * secret_scan says when it has every fragment, and what it put back
    together, and shreds the working directory it made itself — without
    --workdir it left the pieces of a reconstituted secret key under /tmp.
  * pgpid-gen mints an identifier without a passport again, and --verify shows
    the civil status for correction before anything is computed, as the shell
    did. The country keeps the shell's FRA default.
  * Nothing suggests `bl-security` any more: the package does not pull the
    shell libraries in, so a person told to run it would find nothing.
  * Carries the business-card template cert_tobizcard renders, in
    /usr/share/pgpid/svg/card.svg. bash-libs keeps its copy, which bl-pgpid
    still reads; the two paths are disjoint.
  * pgpid-gen and pgpid-qrscan call the actions by the names they have had
    since they were put into groups. Both died on their first real step.

 -- Mnêmê <mneme@foopgp.org>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 15:33:32 +0200

